# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, `core.autocrlf=true` (the default on Windows) silently
# decides for every file, and a later change of mind costs one whole-repository
# renormalisation commit. Setting it up front keeps the history clean.
#
# The rule: normalise to LF in the repository, restore native endings in the
# working tree. Files that Windows tooling expects to be CRLF are pinned.

* text=auto

# MSVC / resource tooling. These are pinned to CRLF so Visual Studio, rc.exe and
# mt.exe see exactly what they wrote.
*.rc        text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj   text eol=crlf

# The four vendored dark-mode files are pinned to LF.
#
# They were copied byte-for-byte out of the win32-dark-mode skill, and UPSTREAM.md
# plus README.md publish their sha256 as the only evidence that they are
# unmodified. With "* text=auto" and core.autocrlf=true, a clone on Windows would
# check them out with CRLF and every published hash would fail -- which is exactly
# the check the documentation tells the reader to run. Pinning them to LF makes
# the hashes reproducible on every platform. This is also why they are compiled at
# /W3: editing them to satisfy /W4 would destroy that evidence.
src/dark/darkmode.h    text eol=lf
src/dark/darkmode.cpp  text eol=lf
src/dark/darkui.h      text eol=lf
src/dark/darkui.cpp    text eol=lf

# Binary.
*.zip       binary
*.png       binary
*.ico       binary
